% ABSTRACT
%========================================================
\clearpage
\phantomsection
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Abstract}

\begin{center}
Vincent Wong\\
INDIVIDUAL COGNITION, SOCIAL STRUCTURE, AND THE EMERGENCE OF GROUP MISPERCEPTION
\end{center}

Misperceptions of social norms --- group expectations for social behavior --- are widespread across domains ranging from dating behavior to climate concern. 
Research on social norm misperception has documented these effects across many domains. However, much of this work focuses on perceptions of groups overall, leaving open the question of how individuals' direct observations of other individuals and their social circles translate into group-level inferences. This dissertation examines how perceptions of individuals shape perceptions and misperceptions of groups through distinct person-perception mechanisms.

The first study uses computational modeling to examine how cognitive biases in sampling and integrating information about others affect emergent perceptions of group norms as people seek groups to join. The model demonstrates that whether collective misperceptions reproduce phenomena in which individuals privately disagree with perceived group norms or exaggerate differences between groups depends critically on how prototypical group members are sampled.

The second study investigates how different social network structures influence inferences about within-group similarity and interpersonal affiliations. Results show that perceived shared interests strongly shape judgments of group similarity and estimates of friendship likelihood, above and beyond the influence of network structure itself.

The third study experimentally examines how inferred reasons for other individuals' behaviors (attributions) transfer to estimates of overarching group opinion. Findings indicate an asymmetric effect in the interaction between perceived behavioral prevalence and attribution tendency: participants underestimate group opinion when a low prevalence of behavior is attributed to beliefs, yet a corresponding change in perceived group opinion does not occur for a high prevalence of behaviors. 

Together, these studies advance a framework for understanding how biases in observing individuals --- through sampling, relational perception, and attribution --- scale up to shape perceptions of groups. This work contributes to research on pluralistic ignorance, social norm misperception, and the cognitive foundations of group perception.

